{"name":"Mountain Meadow Green","hex":"#418638","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elevation of land of considerable dimensions rising more or less abruptly, forming a conspicuous figure in the landscape, usually having a small extent of surface at its summit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something very large in size or quantity; a huge amount; a great heap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain"},{"term":"meadow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A field or pasture; a piece of land either intentionally cultivated with grass or (especially) naturally covered with grass, especially one that is intended to be mown for hay or to be grazed.","Low land covered with coarse grass or rank herbage near rivers and in marshy places by the sea."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cultivate with grass in order to produce hay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meadow"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"},{"title":"Meadow","blocks":[{"text":"A meadow is an open habitat or field, vegetated by grasses, herbs, and other non-woody plants. Trees or shrubs may sparsely populate meadows, as long as they maintain an open character. Meadows can occur naturally under favourable conditions but are often artificially created from cleared shrub or woodland for the production of hay, fodder or livestock. Meadow habitats as a group are characterized as semi-natural grasslands, meaning that they are largely composed of species native to the region, with only limited human intervention."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Blumenwiese_bei_Obermaiselstein05.jpg/330px-Blumenwiese_bei_Obermaiselstein05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meadow","wikidata":"Q7777019"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chimborazo, Ecuador, whose summit is the point farthest away from the Earth's center[5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVolc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png/500px-Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png/1280px-Mountain_regions_identified_by_UNEP.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Mountain_region